{"authored" [] "sources" [{"question" "q-20797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}$ and the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-20797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let $u={x}^{3}$,  then $\\text{d}u=3{x}^{2}\\,\\text{d}x.$ Hence, the antiderivative is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int 3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int {e}^{u}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & ={e}^{u}+C \\\\  & ={e}^{{x}^{3}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{{x}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{({e}^{{x}^{3}})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{{a}^{3}}) \\\\  & =1-0 \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x=1.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }3{x}^{2}{e}^{-|x{|}^{3}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =1+1 \\\\  & =2.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-20797" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e9d6dfc101b7740837fa48c09f0395f689857916a7b5f11fb8b27d068087e648"} {"question" "q-20797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-20797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-20797" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "2"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-20804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-20804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u={x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\int \\frac{1}{9+{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{u}{3}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{x}^{2}}{3})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{0}^{2}}{3}-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(0-\\frac{1}{3}\\operatorname{arctan}⁡\\frac{{a}^{2}}{3}) \\\\  & =0-\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that \n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{2x}{{x}^{4}+9}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{\\pi }{6}+\\frac{\\pi }{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-20804" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e9d6dfc101b7740837fa48c09f0395f689857916a7b5f11fb8b27d068087e648"} {"question" "q-20804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-20804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "3"] ["math" "4"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["text" "The integral is divergent"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-20804" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-30967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Find the total area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve $y=|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ and the $x$-axis." "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-30967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We find the area of the region bounded by the graph of the curve and the $x$-axis by calculating the integral\n\n$$\nA={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nWe break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe start by calculating the first of these integrals:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x=-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=-{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=-2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =-\\frac{1}{2}\\int {e}^{u}\\text{d}u \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{u}+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the integral, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x & =-{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}x{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({-\\frac{1}{2}{e}^{-{x}^{2}}|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({e}^{0}-{e}^{-{a}^{2}}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}(1-0) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand $|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}$ is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{2}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }|x|{e}^{-{x}^{2}}\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}+\\frac{1}{2} \\\\  & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-30967" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e9d6dfc101b7740837fa48c09f0395f689857916a7b5f11fb8b27d068087e648"} {"question" "q-30967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-30967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "1"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{5\\pi }{6}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{\\pi }{3}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-30967" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "1"] "category" "ma_successful_grade" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-118776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate the integral ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-118776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x\n$$\n\nFirst, we calculate the antiderivative using a substitution. Let  $u=3+{x}^{2}$,  then $\\text{d}u=2x\\text{d}x.$ Hence,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & =\\frac{1}{2}\\int \\frac{1}{{u}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{u})+C \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})}+C.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe now evaluate the first of the improper integrals, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{(-\\frac{1}{2(3+{x}^{2})})|}_{a}^{0} \\\\  & =-{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }(\\frac{1}{2(3+0)}-\\frac{1}{2(3+{a}^{2})}) \\\\  & =-(\\frac{1}{6}-0) \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **odd** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{1}{6}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{x}{{(3+{x}^{2})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =-\\frac{1}{6}+\\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-118776" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/moderate "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e9d6dfc101b7740837fa48c09f0395f689857916a7b5f11fb8b27d068087e648"} {"question" "q-118776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :answer-field.type/radio "category" "ma_widget" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-118776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/choices" "value" [["math" "0"] ["math" "2"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{2}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{3}"] ["math" "\\frac{1}{6}"]] "category" "ma_complete_choices" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-118776" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/correct" "value" [:answer.type/math "0"] "category" "model_interpretation" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-118777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/problem" "value" "Evaluate ${\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-118777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/worked-solution" "value" "We break up the integral as follows:\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nEvaluating the first of these integrals, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{2}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }{\\int }_{a}^{0}\\frac{1}{{x}^{2}+{(\\frac{3}{\\sqrt{2}})}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }({\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}x}{3})|}_{a}^{0}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}{lim}_{a\\to -\\infty }[\\operatorname{arctan}⁡0-\\operatorname{arctan}⁡(\\frac{\\sqrt{2}a}{3})] \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\cdot [0-(-\\frac{\\pi }{2})] \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, notice that the integrand is an **even** function. This means that we can immediately deduce that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x=\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, since both improper integrals exist, we conclude that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{-\\infty }^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x & ={\\int }_{-\\infty }^{0}\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x+{\\int }_{0}^{\\infty }\\frac{3}{9+2{x}^{2}}\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}}+\\frac{\\pi }{2\\sqrt{2}} \\\\  & =\\frac{\\pi }{\\sqrt{2}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/state.json" "file_sha256" "22beecdac82fd4971de71066a6fd828298effaceab18e20f6889d031e7fdd42d"} {"question" "q-118777" "field" nil "attribute" "question/difficulty" "value" :question.difficulty/easy "category" "ma_capture" "file" "/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture/14067053/activity-metadata.json" "file_sha256" "e9d6dfc101b7740837fa48c09f0395f689857916a7b5f11fb8b27d068087e648"} {"question" "q-118777" "field" "selection" "attribute" "answer-field/type" "value" :an